From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: X7 Game build <x7game@invalid>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:14:37 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] soc/usb: r36s: RAM check after release and role switch retry
 (X7 Game)

nzeus-ram: avp_check hashed the AVP's areas even after release had given
them to the page allocator. Linux itself writes those pages then, so the
read blocked for 10 s and then usually said CHANGED / AVP_RUNNING, the
opposite of the truth. Once the region is released it now answers
"RELEASED: region now owned by Linux, check not meaningful" at once.

musb hc16xx: hc16xx_add_core() leaves a core that did not start
registered (glue->musb_pdev set, glue->musb NULL), and hc16xx_role_set()
removed it only when the requested mode was host. After a failed switch
to peripheral, or a failed peripheral fallback after a failed host
switch, the next write of "device" hit the early-out, returned 0 and
changed nothing: no UDC, no USB shell. Now every failed add_core() is
followed by hc16xx_del_core(), the fallback's result is checked, and the
early-out needs a started core (glue->musb), so writing the role again
really retries. A failed host switch still returns its error to the
writer, also when the fallback failed too (then no core is left until
the next role write).

After a role round trip the configfs gadget comes back by itself only if
the re-created core gets the same musb-hdrc.N.auto UDC name again;
otherwise userspace has to bind it to the new UDC. That is not part of
this patch.
---
 drivers/soc/nzeus-ram.c   | 11 +++++++++++
 drivers/usb/musb/hc16xx.c | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 2 files changed, 35 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/nzeus-ram.c b/drivers/soc/nzeus-ram.c
index 60d2a9352..3574d118c 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/nzeus-ram.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/nzeus-ram.c
@@ -17,6 +17,8 @@
  *                 twice, 10 s apart. STATIC = nothing wrote them = AVP not
  *                 running. Run it once with the AVP present: it must say
  *                 AVP_RUNNING, which proves the check can see the firmware.
+ *                 After release it only says RELEASED (X7 Game): Linux then
+ *                 owns and writes these pages, so a hash means nothing.
  *   mirror_check  reads the live jiffies counter and a block of kernel text
  *                 at X and X + 128 MiB through KSEG1 (0xA0000000 window).
  *   release       write "release": runs avp_check again and only if STATIC
@@ -165,6 +167,15 @@ static ssize_t avp_check_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
 	bool quiet;
 
 	mutex_lock(&r->lock);
+	if (r->released) {
+		/*
+		 * The page allocator hands these pages out now: Linux itself
+		 * writes them, so the hash would say CHANGED/AVP_RUNNING (after
+		 * a 10 s wait) whatever the AVP does. Don't hash.
+		 */
+		mutex_unlock(&r->lock);
+		return sysfs_emit(buf, "RELEASED: region now owned by Linux, check not meaningful\n");
+	}
 	quiet = avp_static(buf, &len);
 	len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "%s\n", quiet ? "AVP_NOT_RUNNING" : "AVP_RUNNING");
 	mutex_unlock(&r->lock);
diff --git a/drivers/usb/musb/hc16xx.c b/drivers/usb/musb/hc16xx.c
index 0788baead..dfa512600 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/musb/hc16xx.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/hc16xx.c
@@ -29,6 +29,10 @@
  *    reached .init) makes the glue probe return -EPROBE_DEFER, not a hard
  *    error, so it is probed again and the USB rescue gadget still appears
  *    (the original glue never waited for the core to bind).
+ *
+ * X7 Game: a role switch whose new core does not start removes that core
+ * again (also in peripheral mode and for the fallback), so writing the role
+ * again really retries; before, it returned 0 and changed nothing.
  */
 
 #include <linux/clk.h>
@@ -463,7 +467,7 @@ static int hc16xx_role_set(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 {
 	struct hc16xx_glue *glue = usb_role_switch_get_drvdata(sw);
 	u8 mode;
-	int ret = 0;
+	int ret = 0, err;
 
 	switch (role) {
 	case USB_ROLE_HOST:
@@ -478,19 +482,32 @@ static int hc16xx_role_set(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 	}
 
 	mutex_lock(&glue->lock);
-	if (mode == glue->mode && glue->musb_pdev)
+	/* a started core only: glue->musb is set by .init, cleared by .exit */
+	if (mode == glue->mode && glue->musb_pdev && glue->musb)
 		goto out;
 
 	dev_info(glue->dev, "role switch: %s -> %s (re-creating the MUSB core)\n",
 		 hc16xx_mode_name(glue->mode), hc16xx_mode_name(mode));
 	hc16xx_del_core(glue);
 	ret = hc16xx_add_core(glue, mode);
-	if (ret && mode != MUSB_PERIPHERAL) {
-		dev_err(glue->dev, "%s core failed (%d): back to peripheral\n",
-			hc16xx_mode_name(mode), ret);
+	if (ret) {
+		/*
+		 * A core that did not start is still registered (unbound, or on
+		 * the deferred-probe list). Remove it, as probe does, so that
+		 * musb_pdev is NULL and the next write of the same role really
+		 * tries again instead of returning 0 with no core running.
+		 */
 		hc16xx_del_core(glue);
-		if (hc16xx_add_core(glue, MUSB_PERIPHERAL))
-			dev_err(glue->dev, "peripheral core failed too\n");
+		if (mode != MUSB_PERIPHERAL) {
+			dev_err(glue->dev, "%s core failed (%d): back to peripheral\n",
+				hc16xx_mode_name(mode), ret);
+			err = hc16xx_add_core(glue, MUSB_PERIPHERAL);
+			if (err) {
+				dev_err(glue->dev, "peripheral core failed too (%d): no MUSB core until a role is written again\n",
+					err);
+				hc16xx_del_core(glue);
+			}
+		}
 	}
 	/* -EPROBE_DEFER is kernel-internal; the sysfs writer gets "try again" */
 	if (ret == -EPROBE_DEFER)
